{% section-card tone="intro" %}
## What is MySQL?

**MySQL is a relational database server.** It stores structured data in tables, lets
applications read and change that data with SQL, and enforces rules such as unique
keys, relationships, and transactions.

Relational databases matter when the application has facts that must stay correct
together: an order and its line items, an account and its permissions, or inventory
and its reservations. MySQL is a practical choice for these workloads because it has
a mature ecosystem, broad tooling support, and a transactional default engine called
**InnoDB**.

{% callout variant="info" %}
The database is only one part of the system. Schema design, indexes, access patterns,
connection management, replication, backups, and operational discipline determine
whether a MySQL deployment remains fast and recoverable.
{% /callout %}
{% /section-card %}

{% interactive-block id="technology/mysql-performance" /%}

{% section-card accent="blue" %}
## How a MySQL write becomes durable

Start with the request path. A query is parsed and optimized, InnoDB changes pages in
memory, and the transaction log records enough information to recover committed work.
Dirty pages can be flushed to data files later, so every commit does not need to
rewrite each affected table page immediately.

{% system-flow title="Simplified InnoDB write path" caption="The exact timing depends on durability settings and hardware. The key distinction is between committing the transaction log and later flushing changed data pages." %}
{% system-node title="Application" eyebrow="Client" icon="code" tone="blue" %}
Sends SQL through a bounded connection pool.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Parser + optimizer" eyebrow="Plan" icon="search" tone="violet" %}
Validates SQL and chooses an access plan.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Buffer pool" eyebrow="Memory" icon="cache" tone="green" %}
Reads and changes cached InnoDB pages.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Redo log" eyebrow="Commit" icon="repeat" tone="amber" %}
Makes committed changes recoverable after a crash.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Data files" eyebrow="Storage" icon="storage" tone="cyan" %}
Receive changed pages during background flushing.
{% /system-node %}
{% /system-flow %}

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Transactions protect invariants" icon="shield" tone="green" %}
Use a transaction when several statements must succeed or fail as one unit. Keep
transactions short so locks and old row versions do not accumulate.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Indexes trade writes for reads" icon="search" tone="blue" %}
An index can avoid scanning rows, but every additional index consumes storage and must
be maintained during writes. Design indexes from real query shapes.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="The buffer pool is the hot path" icon="cache" tone="violet" %}
Frequently used table and index pages should fit in memory. Disk latency becomes much
more visible when the working set repeatedly misses the buffer pool.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="amber" %}
## Storage engines: default to InnoDB

A storage engine controls how each MySQL table stores data, handles concurrent access,
and recovers after failure. Use InnoDB for modern application tables unless a measured,
specialized requirement makes one of the narrower alternatives a better fit.

{% concept-grid columns="4" %}
{% concept-card title="InnoDB" eyebrow="Default" icon="database" tone="blue" %}
Use for almost every new application table. It provides transactions, row-level
locking, foreign keys, crash recovery, and clustered primary-key storage.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="MyISAM" eyebrow="Legacy" icon="storage" tone="amber" %}
Useful mainly when maintaining an existing MyISAM workload. It lacks transactions and
crash-safe recovery, and table-level locks make concurrent writes difficult.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="MEMORY" eyebrow="Ephemeral" icon="speed" tone="violet" %}
Stores table rows in memory and loses them on restart. Consider it only for bounded,
reconstructable data after comparing it with application or distributed caches.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="ARCHIVE" eyebrow="Specialized" icon="boxes" tone="green" %}
Provides compressed, append-oriented storage with limited access patterns. Object
storage or an analytical system is usually a better fit for modern archives.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

{% callout variant="tip" %}
Default to InnoDB unless a measured requirement demands another engine. Storage-engine
choice is not a substitute for choosing the right database category for the workload.
{% /callout %}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="violet" %}
## Replication improves availability; backups enable recovery

Replication copies committed changes to other MySQL servers for read scaling and
faster failover. Because it also copies accidental deletes and bad application writes,
it cannot restore lost history. Pair replication with independent backups and regular
restore tests.

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Primary + replicas" eyebrow="Common default" icon="repeat" tone="blue" %}
One primary owns writes; replicas serve suitable reads and provide failover candidates.
Asynchronous replication keeps write latency low but allows lag and possible data loss
during an unplanned promotion.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Semi-synchronous replication" eyebrow="Durability" icon="shield" tone="green" %}
The primary waits for at least one replica to acknowledge receipt of the transaction.
This reduces the loss window, but adds network latency and still requires a failover
orchestrator.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Group Replication" eyebrow="Coordinated cluster" icon="network" tone="violet" %}
Members coordinate transaction certification and membership. MySQL InnoDB Cluster can
build on it for managed failover, at the cost of stricter networking and operational
requirements.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

{% tabs %}
{% tab title="Primary + replicas" %}
### Best for a clear write owner

Route writes to the primary. Send only lag-tolerant reads to replicas, and keep
read-after-write traffic on the primary when the user must immediately observe a
change.

{% code-block file="/api/content/technology/mysql/code/primary-replica-architecture.txt" language="text" title="Primary-replica topology" /%}
{% /tab %}

{% tab title="Multiple writers" %}
### Treat write conflicts as a correctness problem

Classic bidirectional replication can create duplicate keys, ordering conflicts, and
surprising failover behavior. Prefer a single writer per data partition unless the
application has a tested conflict model.

{% code-block file="/api/content/technology/mysql/code/primary-primary-architecture.txt" language="text" title="Why dual-writer topology needs care" /%}
{% /tab %}

{% tab title="Failover" %}
### Promotion is only one step

A safe failover verifies replica freshness, prevents the old primary from accepting
writes, promotes one candidate, redirects clients, and rebuilds redundancy. Automate
the workflow and exercise it under load before relying on it.
{% /tab %}
{% /tabs %}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="cyan" %}
## Where MySQL fits in a system

Use workload shape and correctness needs instead of popularity as the selection rule.

{% concept-grid columns="4" %}
{% concept-card title="Transactional core" icon="check" tone="green" %}
Orders, billing records, identity, permissions, and other normalized data with clear
integrity rules are strong fits.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Service-owned metadata" icon="layers" tone="blue" %}
A service can own a focused schema and evolve it through versioned migrations without
sharing write access with unrelated services.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Tenant-partitioned SaaS" icon="users" tone="violet" %}
Partition by a stable tenant key when one cluster no longer meets capacity or isolation
needs. Keep routing and resharding explicit.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Not every access pattern" icon="warning" tone="rose" %}
Large analytical scans, high-cardinality telemetry, vector similarity, and blob
storage usually belong in systems designed for those workloads.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="green" %}
## Optimize with an evidence loop

Performance work should start with a user-visible symptom and end with a measured
result. Tuning random server variables before understanding the query plan often moves
the bottleneck instead of removing it.

{% process-flow %}
{% process-step number="1" title="Observe the symptom" label="Measure" %}
Use latency percentiles, throughput, error rate, lock waits, connection pressure, and
the slow query log to isolate the affected workload.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="2" title="Inspect the plan" label="Explain" %}
Run `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` on a representative query. Check rows examined, chosen indexes,
join order, temporary tables, and sorting work.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="3" title="Fix the access pattern" label="Change" %}
Reduce returned columns and rows, add the narrowest useful index, batch round trips, or
change a query that defeats an existing index.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="4" title="Protect concurrency" label="Load" %}
Bound connection pools, shorten transactions, avoid hot rows, and test with realistic
contention rather than isolated single-query benchmarks.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="5" title="Tune the server" label="Configure" %}
Size the InnoDB buffer pool from the working set and host role. Review redo capacity,
I/O behavior, and connection limits with memory headroom.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="6" title="Verify and watch" label="Prove" %}
Compare before and after plans and latency under production-like load. Add a regression
signal so the same issue is detected early next time.
{% /process-step %}
{% /process-flow %}

{% code-block file="/api/content/technology/mysql/code/explain-index-workflow.sql" language="sql" title="Measure an index change instead of guessing" /%}
{% /section-card %}

